Hi Team,

As part of the review for managing DSRM credentials through CyberArk, I wanted to outline the key concern, available option, and required prerequisites for testing.

The main question is whether the Domain Controllers will allow controlled PowerShell Remoting / WinRM from the approved CyberArk CPM servers.

This is important because, for CyberArk to manage the DSRM password directly, the CPM would need to execute a PowerShell-based process that connects to the target Domain Controller and runs the Microsoft NTDSUTIL command locally on the DC to reset the DSRM password.

If PowerShell Remoting or remote execution to Domain Controllers is blocked by the current hardening process, then this approach will not work unless an approved exception or secure execution model is created.

If this is allowed, the process would work as follows:

CyberArk CPM initiates the password change process.
The CPM connects to the target Domain Controller using PowerShell Remoting / WinRM.
A linked privileged execution account authenticates to the DC.
The DC runs NTDSUTIL locally.
NTDSUTIL runs the following command:

reset password on server null

The DSRM password is then changed on that specific Domain Controller.

The key point is that NTDSUTIL would run on the Domain Controller, not on the CPM server.

For this model, the required network access would be:

Source: CyberArk CPM server(s)
Destination: Domain Controller(s)
Protocol: TCP
Port: 5986
Purpose: PowerShell Remoting over HTTPS

Alternatively, if HTTPS WinRM is not available, the Kerberos-based HTTP option would be:

Source: CyberArk CPM server(s)
Destination: Domain Controller(s)
Protocol: TCP
Port: 5985
Purpose: PowerShell Remoting over HTTP/Kerberos

The firewall rule should be tightly scoped so only approved CyberArk CPM servers can connect to the approved Domain Controllers.

CyberArk would also require a linked execution account. This is not the DSRM account itself. It is the account CyberArk uses to connect to the DC and run the reset process.

The recommended permission model would be:

Delegated DC-local admin model approved by AD/Security

The linked account must be allowed to:

Connect to the DC using PowerShell Remoting / WinRM.
Run the controlled PowerShell process.
Execute ntdsutil.exe locally on the DC.
Reset the DSRM password on that DC.

For CyberArk testing, two account types would be needed:

Target DSRM account per DC
Account Name: DSRM-DC01
Platform: Custom DSRM platform
Address: DC01.domain.com
Username: Administrator
Password: Current DSRM password for DC01
Linked execution account
Account Name: svc_cyberark_dsrm_reset
Platform: Windows Domain Account
Address: domain.com
Username: svc_cyberark_dsrm_reset
Password: Current AD password

The linked execution account would be associated with the DSRM target account as the Logon or Reconcile account.

In summary, Option A can work only if the following are approved:

1. WinRM / PowerShell Remoting from CyberArk CPM to the DC.
2. A linked privileged account allowed to authenticate to the DC.
3. Permission to execute NTDSUTIL locally on the DC.
4. Tight firewall scope from CPM to DC only.
5. Security approval for the CyberArk CPM-driven reset process.

If these controls are not allowed due to Domain Controller hardening restrictions, then CyberArk should not directly execute against the DC. In that case, we should consider the alternative DC-local sync model, where CyberArk manages a domain-based sync account and the DC runs an approved local process to update the DSRM password internally.

Thanks,
Ben
